  "final": "`DocumentService.archive` in `src/service.ts` now enforces the authorization rules. `tsc -p tsconfig.json && node checks/public.cjs` passes, including the new checks.\n\n- **Visibility:** The service looks for a document with the given id that is in the actor's tenant and not deleted. If there isn't one, it throws `Error('not found')`. That covers missing, deleted and foreign-tenant documents, and admins get no exception. It searches `repository.documents` directly rather than using `repository.find`. `find` returns the first row with a matching id, so a foreign-tenant or deleted row with the same id could hide a visible one.\n- **Permission:** An admin may archive any visible document. An editor may archive only documents they own. A viewer, or any unrecognized role, gets `Error('forbidden')`.\n- **Order of checks:** Permission is checked before the already-archived check. A forbidden actor therefore gets `forbidden` even on an already archived document.\n- **Success and repeats:** Archiving an unarchived document goes through the existing `repository.archive`. That sets `archived` to true and appends `{actor: actor.user, document: id}` to the audit log. Repeating the call on an archived document by an authorized actor does nothing.\n- **Failures:** Every check runs before anything is written, so a failed call changes no document and no audit entry.\n\nI appended checks to `checks/public.cjs`. They cover:\n- not-found cases, including admin on deleted, foreign-tenant and missing documents;\n- editor and viewer `forbidden` cases, including on an archived document;\n- state and audit unchanged after failures;\n- successful editor and admin archives;\n- the no-op repeat.\n\nI didn't change any public interfaces.",
